// Logging over the build-selected provider (design.md "Build-selected
// providers"; standard-library "`core:log`").
//
// A `Logger` is an ordinary service handle: a `dyn mut` view of a sink, the same
// shape `fmt.Writer` has. Nothing about it is compiler-known. What the
// program selects is which one the package-level procedures below dispatch to,
// and `-log-level` decides which of them have a body at all.
//
// There is no scoped or package-local override of the program logger. A package
// that needs a different sink, captured test output, or request-specific fields
// takes a `Logger` parameter or keeps one in a state object.
@(public)
package log;

import "core:fmt";
import "core:mem";

// The compiler contributes:
//
//   Level   the severity enumeration, which is also `LOKE_LOG_LEVEL`'s type
//
// It is contributed rather than declared so the two are one type: a `when` over
// `LOKE_LOG_LEVEL <= .Info` compares two values of the same enum, and a second
// nominal type here would make that comparison impossible to write.
//
// Its members, in order, are `Debug`, `Info`, `Warning`, `Error`, and `Off`.
// `Off` is a threshold only — nothing logs at it — and it is last so that
// `LOKE_LOG_LEVEL <= .Error` is false when logging is compiled out entirely.

// One logging sink. Any record with this `write` is one; the message carries no
// line ending.
Sink :: interface($Self: type) {
	slot write: proc(self: inout Self, level: Level, message: string_view);
}

// The handle a program logs through: a view of a sink that lives as long as the
// process, since a factory's result is kept for the life of the program.
Logger :: dyn mut Sink;

// Where a selected logging provider is published. Generated startup writes the
// factory's result here, once, before `main`; until then it is the nil view
// and every call goes to the standard sink below. That is also what makes an
// unselected build need no initialization at all.
@(private)
selected: Logger = nil;

// The handle the program is logging through, for code that wants to pass it on
// rather than call the package procedures.
current :: proc() -> Logger {
	if (selected == nil) { return standard_logger(); }
	return selected;
}

// The standard logger: one line per record on the process error stream, with
// the level in front of it. It is the fallback *and* an ordinary provider — a
// build may select `log.standard_logger` explicitly to say so.
standard_logger :: proc() -> Logger {
	return (Logger)(&mut standard);
}

@(private)
Standard :: struct {}

@(private)
standard: static Standard = {};

impl Standard {
	// ponytail: five writes per record, so records from two threads can
	// interleave within a line. Nothing can start a Loke thread yet; when
	// something can, format the whole line once and write it in one call.
	write :: proc(self: inout, level: Level, message: string_view) {
		w := fmt.stderr();
		fmt.format_to(w, "[");
		fmt.format_to(w, level);
		fmt.format_to(w, "] ");
		fmt.format_to(w, message);
		fmt.format_to(w, "\n");
	}
}

// The four record procedures. Each guards its own body with the compiled level,
// so a call below it never reaches the provider.
//
// Discarding a callee's body does not discard the caller's expressions: an
// unguarded call below the level still checks and evaluates its arguments. To
// remove those too, guard the *call*:
//
//     when (LOKE_LOG_LEVEL <= .Debug) { log.debug("state", expensive()); }

debug :: proc(args: ..any_view) {
	when (LOKE_LOG_LEVEL <= Level.Debug) { record(Level.Debug, ..args); }
}

info :: proc(args: ..any_view) {
	when (LOKE_LOG_LEVEL <= Level.Info) { record(Level.Info, ..args); }
}

warning :: proc(args: ..any_view) {
	when (LOKE_LOG_LEVEL <= Level.Warning) { record(Level.Warning, ..args); }
}

error :: proc(args: ..any_view) {
	when (LOKE_LOG_LEVEL <= Level.Error) { record(Level.Error, ..args); }
}

// Is anything at this level compiled in? A caller with an expensive argument
// asks the constant directly in a `when`; this is for a runtime branch over a
// level held in a variable -- which is the caller who can be handed `Off` from
// configuration, so `Off` answers false rather than comparing: it is a
// threshold, there is no procedure that records at it, and nothing logs there.
enabled :: proc(level: Level) -> bool {
	return level != Level.Off && LOKE_LOG_LEVEL <= level;
}

// A record at a level chosen at runtime, which is what `enabled` above is for:
// having branched on a level held in a variable, this is how a caller records
// at it without switching over five members to reach one of the four fixed
// procedures.
//
// The `when` is the same guard those four carry, asked once for the whole
// package: `<= Error` is false only when logging is compiled out entirely, so
// an `off` build discards this body too. `enabled` then does the per-level
// filtering that a compiled constant cannot, because the level is not one.
at :: proc(level: Level, args: ..any_view) {
	when (LOKE_LOG_LEVEL <= Level.Error) {
		if (enabled(level)) { record(level, ..args); }
	}
}

// The allocation here applies the library's ordinary failure policy, which for
// logging is worth stating rather than assuming: `fmt.to_string` panics when it
// cannot allocate, so a record emitted while handling a failure can end the
// process instead of reporting it. A log call is not a no-op under memory
// pressure. Nothing quieter is available while `core:fmt` has no `try_to_string`
// and these four procedures return nothing to report through.
//
// ponytail: one allocation per emitted record, because formatting into a
// caller-supplied buffer needs a `fmt.Writer` over storage this package would
// have to size arbitrarily, and truncation there would have to cut a rune. If
// logging volume makes this matter, give `Sink` a bytes `write` like
// `fmt.Sink`'s and stream into it instead. A suppressed call allocates
// nothing: its whole body is discarded.
@(private)
record :: proc(level: Level, args: ..any_view) {
	handle := current();
	text := fmt.to_string(mem.default_allocator(), ..args);
	handle.write(level, text);
}
